  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable through mid-week. Some patchy frost\npossible tonight.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage earlier today and associated blustery \nconditions, mainly tranquil weather is expected through mid-week. \nThe pressure gradient will lessen this evening as high pressure \nnudges in from the southwest and remains within vicinity of the Mid-\nAtlantic through Wednesday. As a result, dry conditions are expected \nwith partly to mostly clear skies. \n\nHigh temps through Wednesday will generally be in the low to mid 60s \n(upper 50s in the higher terrain). Low temps the next couple of \nnights will fall into the mid to upper 30s for most of the region \nwith low to mid 40s along the urban corridor and along the coast. \nSuppose some patchy frost is possible where the growing season \nremains active across portions of southern NJ and into the Delmarva. \nHowever, forecast confidence in occurrence is low as there is \nindication of a mid-level cloud deck developing after midnight \ntonight, which would preclude the development of frost. For this \nreason, have not issued any frost advisories at this time.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strengthening storm system will impact the region \nThursday through Friday night, with much colder air on Friday and \nover the weekend.\n\nA vigorous upper-level trough continues to be forecast to evolve \nacross the eastern U.S. later this week and into the weekend. This \nsupports a surface low that strengthens as it tracks across the \nNortheast and Mid-Atlantic region. The main low main tracks into the \nOhio Valley while a secondary low develops in the vicinity of the \nnorthern Mid-Atlantic. The model guidance, including ensemble data, \ncontinues to be in good agreement that a deep upper-level trough \nevolves in the East later this week. There are however some \ndifferences remaining with the handling of the embedded shortwaves \nand thus the details of the surface low. This will determine whether \nthe strengthening storm tracks over our region or slides just south \nand then east/offshore. Despite the continued uncertainty on the \nfiner details, our region will still be impacted by a storm.\n\nThe strength of the parent upper-level trough results in a colder \nair mass moving in with time, however the initial air mass is not \nall that cold (the lack of a cold antecedent air mass). The air mass \nmay become sufficiently cold enough on the west to northwestern edge \nof the precipitation shield for a rain/snow mix or even some snow \nacross the higher elevations of our far western to northwest zones, \nconfined particularly to the southern Poconos. Otherwise, a rain \nevent is forecast. The amount of precipitation (rain and/or snow) \nwill depend on the ultimate development and track of the main \nsurface low. The GEFS and ECMWF members suggest a spread still with \nthe placement/track of the surface low, however overall this looks \nto be mostly a rain event for the majority of our region. The \ncoastal impacts, including the potential for stronger winds and also \ntidal flooding, are less certain because if the surface low tracks \nright over our area that would lessen the residence time of the \n(stronger) onshore flow. If an inland track to the surface low \noccurs, then portions of our area could get briefly into some milder \nair and therefore some thunder would be possible. The strongest \nwinds with this may end up occurring as the storm exits and the cold \nair advection then increases from the northwest. The main \nuncertainty is on how much precipitation occurs with this event and \nthe extent of any higher elevation snowfall (southern Poconos). \n\nIn the wake of the storm, as it deepens and tracks toward the \nCanadian Maritimes over the weekend, a colder air mass will be in \nplace across our area. In addition, a tightened pressure gradient \ncoupled with deeper mixing should result in a period of breezy to \nwindy conditions over the weekend. Daytime temperatures on Saturday \nshould not get out of the 40s for many areas, with temperatures at \nnight dropping into the 20s to low 30s. There could even be some \nlake effect snow and/or rain showers into the southern Poconos on \nSaturday.",
